For the moment the interesting question is if the \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p\) ratio can be used to further reduce the hadronic contamination of the track sample. The left plot of figure 5.21 shows the ratio for particles from cluster pairs where both daughters where previously selected as electrons by the \(\mbox{d}E/\mbox{d}x\) method. The reader should note that for this plot \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}\) has been calculated using the planned 3 towers per cluster, in contrast to the 1 tower per cluster used on the trigger level and discussed so far. A clear peak at \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p\approx 1\) is visible, indicative of electrons. The peak position is shifted to slightly larger values due to the background energy from low momentum particles present in the high-multiplicity environment of a \(Au+Au\) collision.

There are no entries at small \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p\) values as expected from the above discussion on the implicit \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p\) cut made on the trigger level. However several entries which relatively large ratios of more than 2 are present. The origin of these entries can be understood as well from the combination of the selection of high energy clusters on the trigger level and the TPC momentum cut used in the analysis. There is some probability of a random match between a e.g. 2 GeV/c track and a high momentum cluster originating from a different source. This probability is further enhanced due to the correlation of particle production in jets. That this is indeed the case demonstrates the right plot of figure 5.21 which shows the \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p\) ratio for tracks selected as hadrons by the \(\mbox{d}E/\mbox{d}x\) method. A cut of \(\sigma(e)<-4\) has been used which should have removed all electrons (c.f. figure 5.19 and the discussion above and in section 4.1.1). With this hadron selection the narrow peak at \(\sim 1\) disappeared and a broad structure with a maximum around \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p=2\) appeared. The position of the maximum can be understood from the minimum cluster energy selected on the trigger level and the TPC momentum cut. Since both the tower energy spectrum as well as the track momentum spectrum are falling quickly with increasing energy (momentum), the probability for random cluster-track matches is largest for clusters and tracks close to the thresholds. As discussed above this

Figure 5.20: Fraction of tracks which pass the electron \(\mbox{d}E/\mbox{d}x\) cut as function of the cut value in units of standard deviation of the electron \(\mbox{d}E/\mbox{d}x\) resolution.

means a minimum \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p\) value of 1.5 which is shifted to higher values due to the use of 3 tower-clusters in the figure.

The clear difference between the \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p\) plots of particles selected as electrons respectively hadrons by the \(\mbox{d}E/\mbox{d}x\) method clearly demonstrates that the \(\mbox{d}E/\mbox{d}x\) cut is quite efficient in selecting electrons and rejecting hadrons. A futher clean-up seems possible by a maximum cut on the \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p\) ratio. A cut of \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p=2\) has a very high electron identification efficiency8, however removes quite some remaining low momentum tracks which, even if they are accepted by the \(\mbox{d}E/\mbox{d}x\) method, are probably hadrons.

Footnote 8: In section 4.1.2 only the efficiencies for a lower \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p\) cut have been calculated. However from symmetry arguments (both the resolution of \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}\) and \(p\) are expected to be gaussian) the efficiency of an upper \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p\) cut can be easily calculated. Following table 4.2 95% electron efficiency is achieved for an lower \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p\) cut of 0.8. The same efficiency should be achieved for an upper \(E_{\mbox{\tiny cluster}}/p\) cut of 1.25. The choosen cut value of 2 has therefore high efficiency estimated to be \((98\pm 6)\,\%\), even in the presence of background in high multiplicity \(Au+Au\) collisions.

